17. exd5 Bd6 { [%emt 0:03:47] [%think 21699] [%out 22100] [%in 2]
[%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1478] The e5 pawn is currently hanging. The rook on
e1 attacks it along the open e-file, and none of Black's pieces defend it. If
White plays Rxe5, the rook would also hit the bishop on e7, which is undefended
too. 17...Bd6 solves both problems: - **Defends e5.** The bishop covers e5
along the diagonal. - **Blockades the d-pawn.** It stops the passed d5 pawn and
keeps the long f3–b7 diagonal closed, so White's bishop can't get at b7 or a8.
- **Is itself protected.** The queen on b6 defends d6 along the sixth rank. -
**Keeps the bishop pair.** The bishop on c4 still controls d3 and e2, so
White's queen can't come out to those squares. Checking White's main replies: -
**Nd2, hitting c4:** simply 18...Bb5. - **Be4, aiming for Qh5 and Qxh7 mate:**
18...g6 or 18...f5, driving the bishop away. - **Re4, hitting c4:** 18...Bb5 or
trading on b3. In all of these Black stays solid and still has the bishop pair.
} 18. Qd2 a5 { [%emt 0:15:32] [%think 91540] [%out 91923] [%in 2]
